    <node><name>1</name><description>Used sin</description><answertest>NumAbsolute</answertest><sans>ans2</sans><tans>rs</tans><testoptions>1</testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt2-2-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>You used \(\sin {@phi@}^\circ\). In the cross-section the angle at the edge of the parallel is the latitude, and the radius of the parallel is the side next to it: \(r = R\cos\varphi\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>2</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt2-2-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>2</name><description>Whole parallel</description><answertest>NumAbsolute</answertest><sans>ans2</sans><tans>rc</tans><testoptions>1</testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt2-3-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>That is the length of the whole parallel, \(2\pi R\cos\varphi\). The radius is \(R\cos\varphi\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>3</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt2-3-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>3</name><description>Radians</description><answertest>NumAbsolute</answertest><sans>ans2</sans><tans>rr</tans><testoptions>1</testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt2-4-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your calculator is in radians. Set it to degrees.</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>4</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt2-4-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>4</name><description>Proportional</description><answertest>NumAbsolute</answertest><sans>ans2</sans><tans>rl</tans><testoptions>1</testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt2-5-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The radius does not shrink in proportion to the latitude. Use \(r = R\cos\varphi\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt2-5-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>[[if test="is(ans2>6370)"]]No circle on the earth has a radius of more than \(6370\,\text{km}\).[[else]][[if test="is(ans2<=0)"]]A radius is a positive length.[[else]]A parallel of radius \({@ans2@}\,\text{km}\) would be at latitude \({@dispdp(ylat,1)@}^\circ\), not \({@phi@}^\circ\).[[/if]][[/if]] In the cross-section through the poles, the radius of the parallel is the side next to the angle of latitude, and \(R\) is the hypotenuse.</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
